**In a computer.** A railway or flight booking database: thousands of people check seats (readers) while a few bookings change them (writers).

**The problem.** A shared database has **readers** (only read) and **writers** (read and write). Many readers at once are harmless, but a writer needs the data to itself: a writer together with any reader or another writer can leave it inconsistent.

**The solution** (Module 4, slides 69–71) uses a counter and two semaphores:

| Name | Starts at | Job |
|---|---|---|
| `readcount` | 0 | how many readers are reading (an ordinary integer) |
| `mutex` | 1 | protects `readcount` |
| `wrt` | 1 | exclusive access for writers |

```
Writer:                        Reader:
  wait(wrt);                     wait(mutex);
  /* write */                    readcount++;
  signal(wrt);                   if (readcount == 1) wait(wrt);
                                 signal(mutex);
                                 /* read */
                                 wait(mutex);
                                 readcount--;
                                 if (readcount == 0) signal(wrt);
                                 signal(mutex);
```

**The key idea: first in, last out.** The **first** reader locks writers out with `wait(wrt)`; later readers just walk in. The **last** reader to leave lets writers back in with `signal(wrt)`.

**A catch.** If readers keep arriving, `readcount` never falls to 0 and a waiting writer can **starve**. (This version favours readers.)

Quick check: In the reader code, which reader calls wait(wrt)?

